  {
   "_id": 5070,
   "ANSWER": "<p style=\"margin-left:8px\">&nbsp;</p>\n\n<ol>\n\t<li style=\"list-style-type:none\">\n\t<ol>\n\t\t<li style=\"margin-top:8px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:12.55pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:53.95pt\"><span style=\"text-autospace:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><b><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">Simplify the <span style=\"letter-spacing:-.1pt\">Expression:</span></span></b></span></span></span></span></span>\n\n\t\t<ul style=\"list-style-type:circle\">\n\t\t\t<li><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:12.8pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:90.0pt\"><span style=\"text-autospace:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">Given: <span style=\"letter-spacing:-.1pt\">n<sup>2</sup>+n<sup>2</sup>+n<sup>2</sup></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t\t<li><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:12.75pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:90.0pt\"><span style=\"text-autospace:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">Simplify: <span style=\"letter-spacing:-.1pt\">n<sup>2</sup>+n<sup>2</sup>+n<sup>2</sup>=3n<sup>2</sup></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t</ul>\n\t\t</li>\n\t\t<li><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:12.4pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:53.95pt\"><span style=\"text-autospace:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><b><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">Big-O Notation <span style=\"letter-spacing:-.1pt\">Definition:</span></span></b></span></span></span></span></span>\n\t\t<ul style=\"list-style-type:circle\">\n\t\t\t<li><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:12.95pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:90.0pt\"><span style=\"text-autospace:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">f(n) = <span style=\"letter-spacing:-.1pt\">O(g(n))</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t\t<li style=\"margin-left:4px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:12.95pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:92.9pt\"><span style=\"text-autospace:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">if there exist positive constants c and n0 such that f(n) &le; c </span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Cambria Math&quot;,serif\">&sdot; </span></span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">g(n) for all<span style=\"letter-spacing:-.2pt\"> n&ge;n0</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t</ul>\n\t\t</li>\n\t\t<li><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:12.3pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:53.95pt\"><span style=\"text-autospace:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><b><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">Applying the <span style=\"letter-spacing:-.1pt\">Definition:</span></span></b></span></span></span></span></span>\n\t\t<ul style=\"list-style-type:circle\">\n\t\t\t<li><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:12.9pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:90.0pt\"><span style=\"text-autospace:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">In this case, f(n) =3n<sup>2</sup> and we want to show 3n<sup>2</sup> = <span style=\"letter-spacing:-.1pt\">O(n<sup>3</sup>).</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t\t<li><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:12.75pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:90.0pt\"><span style=\"text-autospace:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">Consider <span style=\"letter-spacing:-.1pt\">g(n)=n<sup>3</sup>.</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t</ul>\n\t\t</li>\n\t\t<li><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:12.3pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:53.95pt\"><span style=\"text-autospace:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><b><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">Find c and <span style=\"letter-spacing:-.25pt\">n0:</span></span></b></span></span></span></span></span>\n\t\t<ul style=\"list-style-type:circle\">\n\t\t\t<li><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:12.95pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:90.0pt\"><span style=\"text-autospace:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">We want to find constants c and n0 such<span style=\"letter-spacing:-.2pt\"> that:</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t\t<li><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:12.8pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:90.0pt\"><span style=\"text-autospace:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">3n<sup>2</sup> &le; c </span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Cambria Math&quot;,serif\">&sdot; </span></span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"letter-spacing:-.25pt\">n<sup>3</sup></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t\t<li><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:12.6pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:90.0pt\"><span style=\"text-autospace:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">Divide both sides by <span style=\"letter-spacing:-.25pt\">n<sup>2</sup></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t\t<li style=\"margin-right:51px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:95%\"><span style=\"tab-stops:90.05pt\"><span style=\"text-autospace:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:95%\">This implies c &ge; 3/n. For large enough n, you can choose c as any constant greater than 0, and there exists some n0 such that this inequality holds for all n &ge; n0.</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t</ul>\n\t\t</li>\n\t\t<li style=\"margin-top:1px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:12.45pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:53.95pt\"><span style=\"text-autospace:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><b><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"letter-spacing:-.1pt\">Conclusion:</span></span></b></span></span></span></span></span>\n\t\t<ul style=\"list-style-type:circle\">\n\t\t\t<li><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:12.8pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:90.0pt\"><span style=\"text-autospace:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">Therefore, 3n<sup>2</sup> = <span style=\"letter-spacing:-.2pt\">O(n<sup>3</sup>)</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t\t<li style=\"margin-left:4px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:13.0pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:92.65pt\"><span style=\"text-autospace:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">This shows that n<sup>2</sup>+n<sup>2</sup>+n<sup>2</sup> = <span style=\"letter-spacing:-.1pt\">O(n<sup>3</sup>).</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t</ul>\n\t\t</li>\n\t</ol>\n\t</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p style=\"margin-right:383px; margin-bottom:11px; margin-left:24px; text-indent:19.4pt\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:227%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">2.<b> </b><b><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:227%\">b) Prove that 3n + 5 &ne; &Omega;(n^2). -2.5 marks Big-Omega Notation Definition:</span></span></b></span></span></span></p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:10.5pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:54.0pt\"><span style=\"text-autospace:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">f(n)=&Omega;(g(n)) if there exist positive constants c and n0 such that f(n) &ge; c </span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Cambria Math&quot;,serif\">&sdot; </span></span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">g(n) for all<span style=\"letter-spacing:-.2pt\"> n&ge;n0</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n</ul>\n\n<p class=\"MsoBodyText\" style=\"margin-top:2px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><b><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\">Suppose by <span style=\"letter-spacing:-.1pt\">Contradiction:</span></span></span></b></span></span></span></p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:12.6pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:54.0pt\"><span style=\"text-autospace:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">Assume <span style=\"letter-spacing:-.1pt\">3n+5=&Omega;(n<sup>2</sup>).</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:12.85pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:54.0pt\"><span style=\"text-autospace:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">This means there exist constants c &gt; 0 and n0 such that: 3n + 5 &ge; c </span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Cambria Math&quot;,serif\">&sdot; </span></span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"letter-spacing:-.25pt\">n<sup>2</sup></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:54.0pt\"><span style=\"text-autospace:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">For all n &ge; n0 <span style=\"letter-spacing:-.5pt\">.</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n</ul>\n\n<p class=\"MsoBodyText\" style=\"margin-top:2px\">&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:24px; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><b><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\">Analyze the <span style=\"letter-spacing:-.1pt\">Inequality:</span></span></span></b></span></span></span></p>\n\n<p class=\"MsoBodyText\" style=\"margin-top:1px\">&nbsp;</p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:54.0pt\"><span style=\"text-autospace:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">Consider the inequality 3n+5 &ge; c </span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Cambria Math&quot;,serif\">&sdot; </span></span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"letter-spacing:-.25pt\">n<sup>2</sup>.</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:54.0pt\"><span style=\"text-autospace:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">Divide both sides by n<sup>2</sup>: 3n+5 / n2 &gt;= <span style=\"letter-spacing:-.5pt\">c</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:54.0pt\"><span style=\"text-autospace:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">Simplify the left-hand side: 3n/n2 + 5/n2 = 3/n + <span style=\"letter-spacing:-.2pt\">5/n<sup>2</sup></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:54.0pt\"><span style=\"text-autospace:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">So the inequality becomes: 3/n+5/n<sup>2</sup> &ge; <span style=\"letter-spacing:-.5pt\">c</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n</ul>\n\n<p class=\"MsoBodyText\" style=\"margin-top:2px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><span style=\"position:absolute; margin-left:159px; margin-top:657px; width:35px\"><span style=\"z-index:-1895824384\"><span style=\"left:0px\"><span style=\"height:24px\"><img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:23px; height:16px\" /></span></span></span></span><b><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\">Taking <span style=\"letter-spacing:-.1pt\">Limits:</span></span></span></b></span></span></span></p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:54.0pt\"><span style=\"text-autospace:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">As n approaches infinity, both 3/n and 5/n<sup>2</sup> approach <span style=\"letter-spacing:-.25pt\">0.</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"margin-right:74px; margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:54.05pt\"><span style=\"text-autospace:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">Thus, 3/n+5/n<sup>2</sup> approaches 0, which contradicts the condition that it should be greater than or equal to some positive constant c.</span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n</ul>\n\n<p class=\"MsoBodyText\" style=\"margin-top:2px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><span style=\"position:absolute; margin-left:159px; margin-top:730px; width:35px\"><span style=\"z-index:-1895823360\"><span style=\"left:0px\"><span style=\"height:24px\"><img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:23px; height:16px\" /></span></span></span></span><b><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:115%\"><span style=\"letter-spacing:-.1pt\">Conclusion:</span></span></span></b></span></span></span></p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li style=\"margin-left:32px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"tab-stops:54.0pt\"><span style=\"text-autospace:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt\">Therefore, the assumption that 3n + 5 = &Omega;(n<sup>2</sup>) is false, Hence, 3n+5 &ne; <span style=\"letter-spacing:-.1pt\">&Omega;(n<sup>2</sup>).</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n</ul>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\">&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:32px\">&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:8px;\">&nbsp;</p>\n",
   "COURSE_CODE": "21CSC201J",
