   "QUESTION_DESC": "<p style=\"margin-left:8px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Show that n^2 +n^2 +n^2 = 3n^2 = O(n^3 )</span></span></span></p>\n",
   "SESSION_ID": 108,
   "TYPE": "sq",
   "key": 5070
  },
  {
   "_id": 5071,
   "ANSWER": "<p style=\"margin-left:8px\"><u><strong>A.&nbsp;<span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Answer: </span></span></strong></u></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Given, </span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">f(n) = n4 + 3n3 + 2n+1; g(n) = &Omega; (n3+4). </span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Answer: To find the lower bound of f(n), </span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:48px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">we have to find c and n0 such that </span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:24px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">0 &le; c.g(n) &le; f(n) for all n &ge; n0 </span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:24px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">0 &le; c &times; g (n) &le; f(n) </span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:24px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">0 &le; c &times; g (n) &le; n4 + 3n3 + 2n+1 </span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:24px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">0 &le; 1.(n3+4 )&le; n 4 + 3n3 + 2n+1 &rarr; true, for all n &ge; 1 </span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:24px\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">f(n) = &Omega; (g(n)) = &Omega; (n 3 ) for c = 1, n0 = 1 </span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:24px\">&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:24px\">&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:24px; text-indent:-32.2pt\"><u><strong><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">B. Answer: </span></span></strong></u></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:24px; text-indent:-32.2pt\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">To find upper bound of f(n), </span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:24px; text-indent:-32.2pt\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">To prove that f(n)=3n+2 is O(g(n)) </span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:24px; text-indent:-32.2pt\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">we need to find constants c and n0 such that: </span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:24px; text-indent:-32.2pt\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">f(n)&le;c<span style=\"font-family:&quot;Cambria Math&quot;,serif\">&sdot;</span>g(n)for all n&ge;n0 </span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:24px; text-indent:-32.2pt\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">3n+2&le;c<span style=\"font-family:&quot;Cambria Math&quot;,serif\">&sdot;</span>n for all n&ge;n0 </span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:24px; text-indent:-32.2pt\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">3n+2&le;c<span style=\"font-family:&quot;Cambria Math&quot;,serif\">&sdot;</span>n </span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:24px; text-indent:-32.2pt\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">2&le;(c&minus;3)<span style=\"font-family:&quot;Cambria Math&quot;,serif\">&sdot;</span>n </span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:24px; text-indent:-32.2pt\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">n&ge;2/(c&minus;3) </span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:24px; text-indent:-32.2pt\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">For the inequality to hold for positive n, </span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:24px; text-indent:-32.2pt\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">c&minus;3&gt;0, so we assume c=4. </span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:24px; text-indent:-32.2pt\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Substituting c=4 in the inequality, we get, no=2 </span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:24px; text-indent:-32.2pt\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">With constants c=4 and n0=2, f(n)&le;c<span style=\"font-family:&quot;Cambria Math&quot;,serif\">&sdot;</span>g(n)for alln&ge;n0 </span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:24px; text-indent:-32.2pt\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">this prove that f(n) is O(g(n)) </span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:24px; text-indent:-32.2pt\">&nbsp;</p>\n",
